      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Running Valgrind on a resource-constrained target is like trying to catch a goblin in a lantern-lit room - it's just not feasible. In that case, manual instrumentation and logging are your best bets.\n\n**Manual Instrumentation:**\n\n1. **Use a custom memory tracking function**: Create a function that manually tracks memory allocations and deallocations. This can be as simple as incrementing a counter each time memory is allocated or deallocated. You can then analyze this counter to identify the source of the leak.\n2. **Implement a memory pool**: Create a memory pool that manages a fixed amount of memory. When memory is allocated from the pool, keep track of it and when it's deallocated, mark it as free. This can help you identify memory leaks by tracking the pool's usage over time.\n3. **Use a heap walker**: Write a function that walks the heap, tracking each allocation and deallocation. This can be a time-consuming process, but it can help you identify memory leaks.\n\n**Logging:**\n\n1. **Log memory allocations**: Log each memory allocation, including the size and location of the allocation. This can help you identify patterns in memory usage.\n2. **Log memory deallocations**: Log each",
